# Cyrius Ecosystem — Crate Ports, Tools, Patterns
# Documents lessons from rewriting AGNOS Rust crates in Cyrius.
# Covers: agnostik, agnosys, kybernet, nous, ark, cyrb.

[[entries]]
name = "ecosystem_overview"
description = "The AGNOS ecosystem in Cyrius (v6.5.10 — v6.5.x is an OPEN minor; v6.4.82 was the v6.4.x closeout, v6.3.45 the v6.3.x): 20+ repos, 99 first-party lib/*.cyr stdlib modules, 0 git deps (mabda folded into stdlib at 3.0.1; bayan + ganita data/math carve folded at v6.1.25/.26; lib/async.cyr cooperative-Future runtime v6.3.11). v6.5.x SO FAR: file-scoped `private` / per-item `public` visibility, the v6.5.0 opener, on the new per-fn + per-var ORIGIN-FILE substrate (.0); overload-suffix dispatch made arity-aware, wrong argc now an ERROR (.1); the CYRIUS_IR=3 substrate unblocked — ir_const_fold erased the jump after a folded constant (.2), and IR=3 dispatched `switch` on a stale register, filed as LASE but actually DCE (.5); diagnostics reported the wrong LINE whenever an include was present (.3); vec_sort_by / vec_select_nth — lib/ had NO ordering primitive at all (.4); sys_exit_group, whose absence hung every threaded program's idiomatic exit epilogue, shipped in seven `cyrius init` templates (.6); the syscall-wrapper pass + `#@incdir` file-relative includes + the alloc_reset allocator-invalidation bug (.7); the thread_join lost-wakeup deadlock, thread_create_detached (a fire-and-forget thread leaked its whole 2 MiB stack), the 12-site `i64::MIN` formatter class, the doc/vet/deny/audit fail-open family, and distlib --all/--check (.8); agnos CH_ENDOW + sys_spawn_path_env, the THREE-STATE futex mutex (392 → 48 ns), and the growable arena + exhaustion policy (.9); the distlib .deps sidecar under-reporting that silently switched OFF cyrius's own consumer check, and alloc_via call plumbing 15.1 → 11 ns (.10). v6.4.x HEADLINE: the SIMD compute arc is COMPLETE across all four backends — x86 SSE+AVX2 (.4-.9), aarch64 NEON (.28-.30), Win64 PE value-form params+returns (.31), cx bytecode per-lane (.32). x86 portion: f32v4 128-bit SSE (.4) + f32 matmul fmadd/dot (.5) + integer vectors i8/i16/i32/i64 (.6) + value-form typed int ops & iv_dp8 int8 dot for b1.58 GEMM (.7) + f32v8 256-bit AVX2, the FIRST VEX/AVX the toolchain ever emits (.8) + f32v8 FMA/dot via 3-byte VEX vfmadd231ps (.9); highest SIMD builtin token is 151; structured-descriptor sentinels -2121 (f32v4) / -2153 (f32v8) in the reserved band ≤-2048; x86 VEX backend emitters live in src/backend/x86/float.cyr. v6.4.10 interim: kernel-blocker fix (bare top-level var X[N] silently 8× under-sized when declared after the first bare top-level statement — two-step bootstrap) + distlib per-module read cap 256KB→1MB. Back-half v6.4.x: struct-sid collision fix + f64v2/v4 descriptor fold (.14), absorber-band cleanup (.15), aarch64 transcendentals exp2/atan + Payne-Hanek (.25), Windows PE batch — TerminateProcess timeout-kill + PE closures (.26), agnos O_RDWR + folded-stdlib campaign — sigil 3.10.1 / sakshi 2.4.5 / ganita 1.0.3 / yukti 2.2.9 (.27), aarch64 NEON SIMD Phase 5 — f32v4/f32v8/int vectors + iv_dp8, last SIMD XFAIL removed (.28-.30), Win64 value-form SIMD params+returns (.31), cx SIMD codegen + new cxvm opcodes 0x66-0x68 (.32). Earlier v6.4.x: CYRIUS_MONOMORPH DEFAULT-ON flip (.0, generics no env flag, CYRIUS_MONOMORPH=0 opts out) + sakshi 2.4.4 fold; alloc_reset() zero-on-reset CVE-class info-leak fix all 4 backends (.1); agnos sys_snd_* audio syscall band (.2); f64v2/f64v4 value constructors + splat/lane-extract (.3). v6.3.x ('language trio', closed v6.3.45): closures (.7/.8, DEFAULT-on), generics via monomorphization (.9/.10, was gated CYRIUS_MONOMORPH=1 → NOW default-on at v6.4.0), async/await Futures (.11, still gated CYRIUS_ASYNC=1), W^X userland ELF (.12), initialized-globals cap 1024→4096 (.41), #derive(Serialize) f64 fields (.40); cycc 1,024,552 B, check.sh 128. Fold-in lineage: sandhi (v5.7.0) / vani (v5.8.0) / 6-sibling batch at v5.8.65 (sakshi+patra+sigil+yukti+sankoch+vani-refold) / niyama (v5.9.0) / 9-sibling version-bump cascade at v5.11.5 / bayan (v6.1.25) + ganita (v6.1.26). v6.0.x (closed v6.0.91): native TLS 1.2/1.3 + CYRIUS_TARGET_AGNOS userspace + full Windows PE FFI. v6.1.x (Backend Codegen Multi-Arc, closed v6.1.41): PIE codegen (x86_64+aarch64, --pie), .gnu.hash dynlink migration, the TS/TSX → JS emitter (cycc --emit-js, Phase D), native-TLS-as-default (over sigil, libssl opt-out, v6.1.21), the bayan + ganita data/math carve (v6.1.25/.26), and the Phase-F security-hardening + per-fn local-table relocation pack (v6.1.31-.40). v6.2.0 (Platform Expansion opens): Phase-0 growable-region foundation — fixup_tbl / fn-tables / codebuf migrated from fixed caps to grow-on-demand (ends the cap-raise treadmill), plus cyrius-init CI/release templates aligned to the patra/sigil toolchain workflow. v6.2.1-.5: element-typed `var a: T[N]` arrays (.1); aarch64/macOS annotation-token codegen fix + ecosystem stdlib fold-in (.2); AGNOS net/entropy/clock syscall peer #45-#55 + threading serial-fallback, un-fail-closed getrandom (.3); TLS transport-vtable (`tls_native_set_transport`) for kernel-freestanding TLS (.4); tls_native.cyr split — 5,857-line monolith into a 302-line hub + 6 focused modules, logic-preserving (.5). CLOSEOUT RUN-IN (.73-.82): audit/capacity repair (.73) and the gvar const-fold fix (.74) that surfaced `_cfo` — a const-folding flag that was re-armed but never cleared across an emitted CALL, so the LEFT operand of a 3-term expression was rewound away. It took FOUR sweeps to close: .74 (PARSE_TERM, 17 sites), .80 (`1 - 2 + 3` == 5, PEXPR tier, 16 sites), .81 (`p * 3 + 1` == 4, struct operator-overload dispatch — `mul`/`div` never cleared it where `add`/`sub` did). Alongside: fn_table >8192 P0, six fn-indexed side tables aliasing their neighbours (.75); identifier pool 256->512 KB (.76); the reserved-intrinsic diagnostic — 67 builtin names in the new TOKNAME_BUILTIN table, all of which used to report `got unknown` (.77); audit scope + PEEKT EOF clamp (.78); sankoch 2.7.6 gzip block-boundary corruption, fixed upstream then folded (.79); CVE-32/33/34 unbounded copies from untrusted source (.81); and the TS arena moved off a fixed base that overlapped tok_types entirely, plus agnos #94/#95 closing the syscall band at #82-#95 (.82)."
category = "cyrius"
---
The AGNOS ecosystem is built entirely in Cyrius. Current
toolchain: **v6.5.10** — v6.5.x is an OPEN minor (v6.4.82 was
the v6.4.x closeout, v6.3.45 the v6.3.x), cycc self-host
at **1,141,792 B** (~1.09 MB) byte-identical on x86_64 + aarch64
(cross+native) + macho-arm + macho-x86 + Windows PE, plus the cx
portable `.cyx` target (now a first-class SIMD-correctness oracle
on all 4 hosts) (self_compile 648 / 652 ms; check.sh **162 gates**
+ QEMU kernel boot gate). Native UEFI Authenticode PE signing landed
as `cyrius sign-efi` (v6.4.47).

⚠ On the bench number: two figures are quoted because the
run-to-run spread on this box is WIDER than the release-over-release
delta (6.5.7 posted 649 / 670 / 701 ms for the SAME binary). A
single self_compile number from this series carries no signal and
should not be quoted as one.

v6.5.x so far (an OPEN minor, .0 through .10):
- **.0 — file-scoped `private` / per-item `public`.** Opt-in per
  file; covers fns AND global vars; a file with no declaration is
  unchanged. The real work was the substrate: the compiler did not
  track which source file a `fn` came from. `_fnt_fileid`
  (fn-indexed) and `_var_fileid` (vcnt-indexed) both lazy-alloc at
  their table's MAX cap rather than taking fixed heap bands — no
  heap-map line, no layout change, no two-step bootstrap. The
  private marker itself is `fn_flags` bit 6 (= 64). Enforcement
  runs through ONE shared check: the plan named two resolution
  sites, there turned out to be at least thirteen. `private` holds
  at LINK time too — a private fn no longer reaches the exported
  dynamic symbol table.
- **.1 — overload-suffix dispatch was arity-blind.** The redirect
  decided on argument 1 and never checked the target's arity, and
  `PARSE_RETURN`'s tail-call path skipped dispatch entirely, so a
  `return` of an overloaded call had been running the wrong
  function for as long as the feature existed. A wrong argument
  count is now an ERROR, not a warning.
- **.2 / .5 — the `CYRIUS_IR=3` substrate unblocked.**
  `ir_const_fold` erased the jump that followed a folded constant
  (`EJCC` / `EJMP0` were the only two emitters recording AFTER the
  bytes); `_read_env`'s shared buffer made every IR diagnostic knob
  silently inert; and IR=3 dispatched `switch` on a stale register,
  filed as a LASE bug but actually DCE — an `IR_RAW_EMIT` marker
  only shields raw bytes until the NEXT recorded node.
- **.3 — diagnostics reported the wrong LINE** once any `include`
  was present (`actual_line` minus the number of preceding
  includes). Markers are now `#@file "NAME" BASE`. The marker
  FORMAT changed, so the seed gate was load-bearing: cybs parses it.
- **.4 — `vec_sort_by` / `vec_select_nth`.** lib/ had no ordering
  primitive at all; ~26 first-party repos each hand-rolled one.
- **.6 — `sys_exit_group`.** Its absence hung any program that had
  spawned a worker and exited through `syscall(SYS_EXIT, r)` — and
  that epilogue shipped in SEVEN `cyrius init` templates, so it was
  the default a consumer inherited.
- **.7 — syscall-wrapper pass + `#@incdir`.** `sys_chdir` was
  CALLED by `lib/regression.cyr` and defined nowhere; `alloc_reset()`
  left the memoized default allocator pointing at freed memory
  (reproducible SIGSEGV); aarch64 `fchownat` was unreachable, fixed
  via a NEW **≥1000 private-alias band** that no OS mints
  (`SYS_FCHOWNAT = 1054` = 1000 + the native number). `cyrius build`
  resolved every `include` against the process CWD, so a source in a
  subfolder could not include its own neighbour — the entry file's
  directory now rides in-band as `#@incdir` on line 1 (`#` is a
  comment, so older cycc / cybs / cx / JS skip it untaught).
  ⛔ In-band means a hostile `.cyr` can write one: the directory must
  be relative and `..`-free, and it is read ONLY at byte 0.
- **.8 — the repair release.** `thread_join` lost-wakeup deadlock
  (it read the tid word TWICE per iteration; permanent, silent,
  ~1 join in 150k, SIGKILL the only recovery); `thread_create_detached`
  + `thread_is_done` (100 unjoined threads leaked 210,124,800 B of
  VA — exactly 100 × 2,101,248 — now 0); the **`i64::MIN`
  formatter class at 12 sites, not the 7 the roadmap named** —
  `n = 0 - n` is a no-op at `i64::MIN`, so every decimal formatter
  emitted the sign byte and nothing else, and bayan then serialised
  `{"n":-}`, which is not JSON, with no error at any layer (two of
  the 12 were inside the compiler itself); `cyrius coverage`'s four
  defects, every one of which printed a number and exited 0; the
  `doc` / `vet` / `deny` / `audit` / `coverage` fail-open family;
  `distlib --all` / `--check`.
- **.9 — the three-state futex mutex** (`lib/sync.cyr`): a 0/1 cell
  cannot tell the releaser whether anyone is parked, so `mutex_unlock`
  entered the kernel on EVERY release — **392 → 48 ns** per
  uncontended pair. ⭐ The roadmap said it was blocked on
  `atomic_swap`; it was not — a successful boolean `atomic_cas(m,1,0)`
  already proves the pre-value was exactly 1. Plus the growable
  arena + exhaustion policy (`ARENA_FULL_NULL` / `GROW` / `SPILL` /
  `ABORT`), and agnos `CH_ENDOW` + `sys_spawn_path_env`.
- **.10 — the `distlib` `.deps` sidecar under-reported.** It scanned
  for literal `include` lines only, so it captured what a module
  INCLUDES and never what it REFERENCES through the declaration.
  ⭐ That did not merely mislead a reader: `cyrius deps` VALIDATES a
  consumer against the sidecar, so a wrong sidecar switched the
  machine check OFF. Base bundle only — a profile's true dep set is
  a subset, so profiles keep the pruned inference. Plus `alloc_via`
  call plumbing: **15.1 → 11 ns**.

The v6.4.x SIMD compute arc is COMPLETE on all
four backends — x86 SSE+AVX2 (.4-.9), aarch64 NEON (.28-.30),
Win64 PE value-form params+returns (.31), and cx bytecode per-lane
(.32) — landing f32v4/f32v8 vectors, integer vectors, FMA/dot, and
the int8 dp8 BitNet inner loop; the x86 side was the first VEX/AVX
instructions the toolchain ever emits. Back-half v6.4.x (.49-.72,
one-glance): Intel-Mac x86_64 Mach-O toolchain revival (.59),
DX diagnostics — column/caret + multi-error panic-mode recovery
(.60-.62), Win64 stack-args P0 (ECALLPOPS 10+-arg corruption fix,
.64), f64 JSON round-trip via bayan Grisu2 (.69), and the AGNOS
GPU syscall band now contiguous (.70-.72, and closed at #82-#95
by .82's gpu_recover_op / uptime_us wrappers) alongside
`cyrius coverage` reporting project src/ not vendored stdlib (.72).
The closeout run-in (.73-.82) was dominated by `_cfo` — a
const-folding flag that was re-armed but never cleared across an
emitted CALL, silently rewinding the LEFT operand of a 3-term
expression. It took FOUR sweeps to close (.74 PARSE_TERM 17 sites,
.80 `1 - 2 + 3` == 5 at the PEXPR tier 16 sites, .81 `p * 3 + 1`
== 4 in struct operator-overload dispatch), each of which had been
called complete. The lesson the retros keep: grep the SHAPE, not
the operator — and a byte-identical 251/251 corpus run means the
corpus has NO coverage of the shape, not that the shape is safe.
Also in the run-in: the fn_table >8192 P0 (six fn-indexed side
tables aliasing their neighbours, .75), identifier pool 256->512 KB
(.76), the 67-name reserved-intrinsic diagnostic (.77), sankoch
2.7.6's gzip block-boundary corruption fixed upstream then folded
(.79), CVE-32/33/34 unbounded copies from untrusted source (.81),
and the TS arena moved off a fixed base that overlapped tok_types
entirely (.82).
The v6.3.x "language trio" minor shipped closures,
generics via monomorphization (was gated CYRIUS_MONOMORPH=1; the
default-on flip SHIPPED at v6.4.0 — now CYRIUS_MONOMORPH=0 opts
out), and async/await Futures (still gated
CYRIUS_ASYNC=1). v5.x closed
at v5.11.69 (70 slots); v6.0.x closed at v6.0.91 (91 patches —
native TLS 1.2/1.3 client+server, AGNOS userspace target, the
full Windows PE FFI arc); the v6.1.x cycle (Backend Codegen
Multi-Arc) shipped PIE position-independent codegen on both
arches (--pie), the .gnu.hash dynamic-link migration, Phase D
— the TS/TSX → JS emitter (`cycc --emit-js`) — native-TLS-as-default
(over sigil, libssl opt-out, v6.1.21), the bayan + ganita data/math
carve (v6.1.25/.26), and the Phase-F security-hardening + per-fn
local-table relocation pack (v6.1.31-.40).

v5.11.x highlights (back half — gnoboot MVP unblocker +
diagnostic-surface polish):
- **v5.11.56-.59 — Iron-boot papercut 4-slot arc**: 4-item
  filing 2026-05-16 closed cleanly across the absorber band.
  .56: LSP now forks `cyrius check --with-deps` wrapper (was:
  raw cc5, no [deps.*] resolution) — closes stdlib-fn false-
  positive class; undef-fn warning wording downgraded across
  x86 + aarch64. .57: cc5-side `_check_cyml_pin_drift()`
  surfaces toolchain drift between cyml pin and cc5 version
  (CYRIUS_NO_WARN_PIN_DRIFT / CYRIUS_STRICT_PIN env knobs);
  shadow-lib note rewritten as content-compare. .58: wrapper
  polish — `cyrius lib sync`, `--strict-pin`, `--version`
  manifest-pin line; version-bump.sh fix for the wrapper-
  rebuild bug that froze ~/.cyrius/bin/cyrius at .25-embedded
  since 2026-05-12. .59: DCE-aware reachability filter cross-
  arch (aarch64 gained full DCE bitmap pass for first time —
  mirrors x86 since v5.10.x).
- **v5.11.51-.53 — Byte-array literal + efi_main convention**:
  `var foo[N] = { 0x.., ... };` literal syntax (.51 — feeds
  gnoboot's EFI GUIDs and UTF-16LE strings). `fn efi_main(
  handle, st)` entry convention (.52) with v5.11.53 hotfix
  for MR-form REX prefix typo (`0x4C → 0x49` in entry-save).
- **v5.11.47-.49 — UEFI Application PE emit (gnoboot arc KO)**:
  3-slot arc enabling cyrius-compiled UEFI applications.
  Subsystem=0xA, no kernel32 imports, single-byte EEXIT (ret),
  NX_COMPAT forced, RELOCS_STRIPPED cleared. New
  `programs/efi_probe.cyr` (64 LoC) boots end-to-end under
  qemu+OVMF writing "hello, uefi" to firmware ConOut. Unblocks
  the sovereign UEFI bootloader gnoboot (~2000 LoC of cyrius
  replacing GRUB on the AGNOS boot path).
- **v5.11.43-.46 — ELF64 + multiboot2 kernel emit (Path A)**:
  EMITELF64_KERNEL with EFI64-entry tag for GRUB-EFI long-mode
  handoff. v5.11.46 fixed FIXUP↔EMITELF64_KERNEL entry-arithmetic
  mismatch. Path A subsequently abandoned at iron-test time due
  to GRUB-internal W^X bug; replaced by Path C (gnoboot).
- **v5.11.35-.39 — Parser-to-emit named-op refactor + drift gate**:
  per-backend EMIT_<OP> helpers replace direct EB(S, 0xXX) byte
  emits in parse_*.cyr; drift gate locks the pattern.
- **v5.11.5 — 9-sibling foldin cascade**: vani/patra/agnosys/
  sandhi/sakshi/sigil/yukti/sankoch/niyama all version-bumped
  to current; single-slot byte-identical re-vendor.

Earlier cycle highlights (v5.10.x): REAL TYPE SYSTEM 5-phase arc,
typed-simd value-type ABI 11-phase arc, TLS early-data surface
completion, sandhi 1.1.0→1.3.3 fold at v5.10.34.

Live `[deps.*]`: **NONE — 0 git deps** as of v6.5.4. mabda was the
last one standing and is now folded like the rest (4.0.8). All twelve
siblings ship as byte-identical distfiles in lib/ (verified against
the vendored headers at v6.5.10): sigil 3.12.2,
patra 1.12.12, sankoch 2.7.6, sakshi 2.4.8, bayan 1.4.1, ganita 1.0.4,
mabda 4.0.8, niyama 1.0.6, yantra 1.0.2, vani 1.1.3, sandhi 1.9.9,
yukti 2.3.2 — verify any of them against the header of the vendored
`lib/<name>.cyr`, and note sakshi uses a DIFFERENT header format
(`# Bundled distribution of sakshi v2.4.8`) that a `# Version:`-only
grep silently skips. sakshi 2.4.8 + bayan 1.4.1 both landed at
cyrius v6.5.8, fixed upstream first. Fold-in lineage: sandhi (v5.7.0), vani (v5.8.0),
sakshi/patra/sigil/yukti/sankoch (v5.8.65 sandhi-pattern batch),
niyama (v5.9.0; 5 regex engines), 9-sibling version-bump cascade at
v5.11.5, bayan + ganita carve (v6.1.25/.26).

These are the language's OWN stdlibs, not external upstream. Full
stdlib discipline applies — and a fix applied only to cyrius's
vendored copy EVAPORATES at the next re-vendor. Patch the source
repo, version-bump it, regenerate the dist (every sub-profile, not
just the main bundle), then re-vendor.

Core ecosystem (all Done):
1. agnostik (6 modules, 58 tests) — shared types: error, lifecycle, security, audit, config
2. agnosys (20 modules) — Linux kernel interface: syscalls, security, udev, netns, tpm, etc.
3. kybernet (7 modules, 140 tests) — PID 1 init: console, signals, reaper, privdrop, mount, cgroup
4. nous — dependency resolver: marketplace + system package resolution
5. ark — package manager CLI: install/remove/search/list/info/status/verify
6. argonaut (424 tests) — serde-style Cyrius type (de)serialization via #derive(Serialize)

Crypto & data (all Done):
7. sigil 3.12.2 (adds Authenticode VERIFY; fixes the unaligned-image hash range) — Ed25519 trust, SHA-256/512, HMAC, HKDF, SHAKE-128/256,
   AES-NI, SHA-NI (v2.9.0 via lib/keccak.cyr from cyrius v5.4.15). AES-NI shipped
   as of sigil 2.9.1 — v5.5.21 fixed the root-cause SSE m128 alignment bug (arrays
   were at 8-byte VAs, SSE needs 16). SHA-NI staged in 2.9.2 (CPUID probe +
   dispatch), compress landed in 2.9.3 (~80x throughput win on x86_64 SHA-NI
   hosts; surfaced by sit v0.6.4's perf review). Bumped 2.9.0 → 2.9.3 at
   cyrius v5.6.43. sigil 3.0 unblocked by v5.5.37 fixup-cap raise (16384 → 32768).
8. patra (v1.12.12) — SQL database: B-tree, WAL, transactions, flock concurrency, blob support (added v1.6.0 at cyrius v5.6.20 for sit consumer); INSERT OR IGNORE + STR-keyed B+ tree indexes + 4KB page-slab allocator + prepared statements (`patra_prepare`/`patra_exec_prepared`/`patra_query_prepared`/`patra_finalize`) bumped 1.6.0 → 1.8.3 at cyrius v5.6.40. **v5.7.9 (BREAKING, minor bump) renamed `fn json_build/6` → `fn patra_json_build/6`** to clear the silent cross-module collision with `lib/json.cyr::json_build/1` (the general pairs-vec utility); cyrius v5.7.9's new duplicate-fn warning surfaces this kind of collision at registration. Consumer migration: rename `json_build(buf, max, keys, vals, types, n)` calls to `patra_json_build(...)`. The 7-arg `json_build_lens` is unchanged.
9. libro (v1.0.1, 240 tests) — cryptographic audit chain: SHA-256 hash-linked event log
10. sakshi (v2.4.8) — structured tracing/logging (slim + full profiles); v6.4.0 added 128-bit W3C trace-id (sakshi_trace_set_128 / sakshi_trace_id_hi / sakshi_trace_id_lo); 2.4.8 (cyrius v6.5.8) fixed the `_sk_fmt_int` half of the `i64::MIN` formatter class

Device/hardware (Done):
11. yukti (v2.3.2, 658 tests) — device abstraction: USB, optical, block, GPU (via /sys/class/drm),
    network, udev hotplug, mount/eject. v5.4.11 unblocked aarch64 syscall
    portability (generic syscall table, at-family wrappers).

Media & apps (Done):
12. shravan — audio codec: FLAC encode/decode, Ogg/Opus, WAV, AIFF (uses lib/audio.cyr ALSA)
13. cyrius-doom — DOOM port (137KB binary, all 9 shareware maps, WAD-native HUD, ALSA audio)
14. bsp (74 tests) — BSP tree rendering
15. tarang — media pipeline
16. majra, nous — additional tooling

GPU foundation (v4.0.8 folded into lib/mabda.cyr — no longer a git dep):
17. mabda (v4.0.8) — GPU foundation via wgpu-native C shim + native NVIDIA/nouveau
    backend and compute dispatch. Was the last live `[deps.mabda]` git dep; it is
    now folded byte-identical into lib/mabda.cyr like the other eleven siblings.
    Historically activated in cyrius.toml at 3.6.3 (the same release that cc3's
    input_buf was expanded from 128KB → 256KB to fit the 141KB dist/mabda.cyr
    bundle).
    v2.0.0 = Rust→Cyrius port. v2.1.x = feature-complete against Rust v1.0 +
    stdlib inclusion + repo hygiene. v2.5.0 = hashmap u64-key unlock (v5.5.20),
    cache-hit path parity with Rust, dep wiring hardened (v5.5.34 closed the
    cross-repo symlink bug that was stripping lib/dynlib.cyr). Backend is
    transitional — wgpu-native + C launcher today, pure Cyrius DRM/KMS driver
    eventually. Public API is the stability contract. Used by: soorat, rasa,
    ranga, bijli, aethersafta, kiran.

In progress:
- bhava, hisab, avatara — in progress ports
- ai-hwaccel (v1.2.0, 18 modules, 491 tests, 6 fuzz) — ported, needs majra+libro for full parity
- hadara (v1.0.0, 330 assertions) — ported, needs entry point fix + dep resolution
- vidya MCP — blocked on bote

Build tool: cyrius (written in Cyrius, 30+ subcommands).
  `cyrius build` auto-resolves deps from cyrius.cyml (legacy cyrius.toml is
  still read as a fallback), prepends includes, compiles.
  `cyrius deps` manually resolves [deps] section: stdlib → lib/{name}.cyr,
  named deps → lib/{depname}_{basename} (namespaced, no collisions).
  `cyrius build -v` verbose mode (compiler path, binary size on stderr).
  `cyrius audit` runs self-hosting + heap audit + .tcyr suite + fmt + lint.

Version manager: cyriusly (formerly cyriusup — renamed in v3.4.15 as "Language Yare").
    cyriusly list    — show installed versions
    cyriusly use X   — switch active version
    cyriusly install X — fetch a new version
    cyriusly update  — check for new releases

Cyrius stdlib: 99 lib/*.cyr modules, 0 git deps (sibling distfiles folded
byte-identical, sandhi-pattern; mabda/bayan/ganita carved in-tree). v5.5.0 added Windows peers (lib/syscalls_windows.cyr,
lib/alloc_windows.cyr) selected via the CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN flag that
cycc_win predefines. v5.5.26-28 added the NSS-free identity quartet
(pwd/grp/shadow/pam) for glibc-NSS-SIGSEGV-free auth; v5.5.28/34
added lib/fdlopen.cyr + the C helper for full foreign-dlopen of glibc.
v5.5.30 added lib/thread_local.cyr (%fs / TPIDR_EL0 runtime slots);
v5.5.31 added lib/atomic.cyr (5 primitives + race-free mutex);
v5.5.33 added lib/flags.cyr (getopt-long CLI parser).

Compiler: cycc 1,141,792 B x86_64 (v6.5.10), 685,312 B aarch64 cross
(cycc_aarch64), 1,021,440 B Windows PE cross (cycc_win; native
self-host byte-identical on Win11 since v5.5.10), 606,104 B cx
bytecode (cycc_cx), Mach-O ARM64 cross (cycc_macho since v5.7.x;
size not re-measured — the macho cross is built on
demand, not tracked in build/). The three cross sizes above are the
artifacts from this release's local cross build. Renamed from cc3 → cc5 at
v5.0.0; per CLAUDE.md discipline the binary name is now version-
agnostic and stays `cycc` forever (no cc6 at v7, no cc7 at v8).
v6.0.0 bundles the cc5 → cycc + cyrc → cybs rename as the final
name-change penalty paid.
v5.5.37 raised fixup cap 16384 → 32768 (fixup_tbl moved to 0x150B000);
v5.5.38 split parse.cyr/lex.cyr via nested `include` (byte-identical);
v5.5.39 retired src/cc/ + src/compiler*.cyr (3,333 LOC of legacy cc3).
growable codebuf (3 MB init, 64 MiB ceiling), 512KB tok_names (256->512 KB
at v6.4.76), growable fn-tables (8192 init, x2 growth, 32768 ceiling),
growable var family (8192 init, x2, 1,048,576 ceiling), 1M tokens,
growable fixups (1,048,576 entries x 16 B init);
**100 heap-map regions, 0 overlaps** at v6.5.10
(fn-tables/fixup_tbl/codebuf grow-on-demand since v6.2.0).
v6.5.0's two visibility side tables (`_fnt_fileid`, `_var_fileid`)
took NO heap-map line — both lazy-alloc at their table's max cap,
deliberately, so the feature needed no layout change and therefore
no two-step bootstrap.

WHAT THE HEAP-MAP NUMBER MEASURES: `sh tests/heapmap.sh` reads the
`#   0xOFFSET  name  [BYTES]` comment block at the top of src/main.cyr —
that block IS the authoritative map — and counts one region per line,
then checks every [base, base+size) pair for intersection. So the number
is "documented regions", and the pass/fail is "the documented layout is
internally consistent". It does NOT prove code writes where the map says:
v6.4.81 found a scratch buffer documented at 0x190500 [256] that no code
had ever written, while the live unbounded one sat at 0x190400.

This line previously read "135 heap-map region entries / 56 live regions",
attributed to the v6.3.45 closeout. That figure matches NO release in this
project's history (checked at 5.8.0 / 5.11.68 / 6.0.0 / 6.1.0 / 6.2.0 /
6.3.0 / 6.3.45) and there is no "live regions" sub-count in the tool's
output — the audit reports one number. It is corrected here, not carried.

The live count's own history is worth a caveat: 100 through v6.4.74, then
94 at .75 (six fn-indexed side tables moved to lazy alloc and their map
lines became FREED markers), then 100 again at .81 — when the auditor's
size parser was fixed to see unit-suffixed entries it had been silently
dropping. Same number by a DIFFERENT route; today's 100 is not the
pre-.75 100. Features (additive since 4.0): +=/-=/*=,
negative literals, numeric underscores, #derive(accessors), native multi-
return, switch case blocks, defer (all exit paths), undefined function
diagnostic, expression-position comparisons, #assert, Str auto-coercion,
string interning, switch jump tables, u128 hardware fast-path, #regalloc
per-fn hot-local routing, capacity meter, `stack var`, `var p: Point;`,
`fn f(): Point` struct-return-by-value (SysV + Win64), `fn f(a, ...)`
variadic (Phase 3-min), `#ifplat`, `cycc --strict`.
Test coverage (v6.5.10, counted in-repo): 260 tests/tcyr/*.tcyr,
6 fuzz/*.fcyr harnesses, 18 benches/*.bcyr, 97 programs/**/*.cyr,
99 lib/*.cyr, api-surface 4817 public fns, 41 shell gate scripts in
tests/*.sh.
check.sh: **162 gates passed / 0 failed** on x86_64. (The "19/19"
that used to sit here was the v5.5.x-era gate list — build + test +
shared + linker + capacity + format + lint + thread-safety + aarch64
cross + ifplat + inline-asm + cyrfmt-write + pwd/grp + regression +
fdlopen + varargs + struct-return + flags + PE reloc. The gate count
grows every minor, which is why the closeout procedure says to RECORD
it rather than restate a list.)

⚠ The shell-gate count is a DIFFERENT number from check.sh's gate
count, and it has been miscounted repeatedly. There are **41** files
in `tests/*.sh`; **31** are registered by exact path in
`programs/checks/main.cyr` and the other **10** run via named gate
fns (`_heapmap_gate` and friends) — ALL 41 are live. Several docs
said "10" or "19", a figure that was wrong at the outset and then
kept being incremented. Count `ls tests/*.sh | wc -l`; do not
increment a remembered number.

VERIFICATION CAVEAT: check.sh's grep summary masks .tcyr segfaults and
exit-code failures. A per-file exit-code loop is what actually proves
the suite green — do that before repeating any number from this file.

Recommended minimum: >= 4.8.4 — PP_IFDEF_PASS 256 KB scan-blindness fix
(post-GA retag) is critical for any consumer whose transitive include
graph expands past 512 KB (bote 2.5.0 tripped this). Below that floor
large units can fail with a misleading `lib/assert.cyr:3: expected '=',
got string` parse error. v4.8.5 adds math stdlib (u64_mulmod /
u64_powmod, inverse trig with correct atan2 quadrants, inverse
hyperbolic, f64 constants) + CRLF hardening in lib/http.cyr.
Projects needing GPU: pin >= 3.6.3. Projects needing multi-return or
switch blocks: pin >= 3.7.4. Projects using u128 mulmod hot-path: pin
>= 4.8.5.

[[entries]]
name = "rewrite_methodology"
description = "How to port a Rust crate to Cyrius: idiom translation, not 1:1 porting"
category = "cyrius"
---
Porting Rust to Cyrius is RE-IMPLEMENTATION, not direct translation.

KEY PRINCIPLE: Strip the type system to what consumers actually need.
Rust agnostik is 7090 lines. Cyrius agnostik is 629 lines (11x less).
Same functionality for the kernel/init/tools that use it.

TRANSLATION PATTERNS:

Rust enum with data → Cyrius integer enum + separate error string functions
  Rust:   AgnostikError::AgentNotFound(String)
  Cyrius: ERR_AGENT_NOT_FOUND = 1001; fn err_print(code) { ... }

Rust struct with derives → Cyrius heap-allocated struct with accessors
  Rust:   #[derive(Clone, Debug, Serialize)] struct Config { name: String, ... }
  Cyrius: fn config_new() { var c = alloc(SIZE); ... } fn config_name(c) { return load64(c); }

Rust HashMap → Cyrius vec of key-value pairs (or sorted vec for lookup)

Rust Result<T> → Cyrius packed Result: Ok(val) returns val, Err(ptr) sets bit 63.
  is_ok(r) = r >= 0. payload(r) = r & 0x7FFFFFFFFFFFFFFF. Zero alloc on success.
  Error structs (kind+errno+msg) heap-allocated only on error path (cold).

Rust Option<T> → Cyrius heap-allocated tagged union: Some(val)/None() via tagged_new.
  Or simpler: pointer (0 = None, nonzero = Some) for lightweight cases.

Rust traits → Cyrius concrete functions (no vtable, no dynamic dispatch)

Rust generics → Cyrius monomorphized (Vec is always Vec<i64>)

Rust String → Cyrius null-terminated C strings (pointers)

Rust serde JSON → Manual parsing (find key, extract value between quotes)

WORKFLOW: Survey Rust source → identify consumed API surface → implement only what's used.

[[entries]]
name = "enum_init_ordering"
description = "CRITICAL: Enum init code must run before global var inits in cc2.cyr"
category = "cyrius"
---
BUG: Enum values were always 0 inside functions.

ROOT CAUSE: In cc2.cyr, the init ordering was:
  1. EMIT_GVAR_INITS (global var initializers — may call functions)
  2. Enum scan (emit enum init code)

A global var like `var r = main()` would call main() during step 1,
but enum values weren't initialized until step 2. So any enum
referenced inside a function returned 0.

FIX: Swap the order in cc2.cyr (around line 241):
  1. Enum scan FIRST (initialize all enum constants)
  2. EMIT_GVAR_INITS SECOND (now functions can see enum values)
  3. STI(S, gvar_save_ti) to restore token index for PARSE_PROG

VERIFICATION: Self-hosting still byte-identical. All 94 tests pass.
Enum values now work correctly inside functions.

[[entries]]
name = "comparison_in_args"
description = "FIXED (v0.9.0): comparisons in function call arguments now work"
category = "cyrius"
---
FIXED in v0.9.0: f(x == 1, y > 2) now works.

The fix: PARSE_FNCALL calls PARSE_CMP_EXPR (not PARSE_EXPR) for each argument.
PARSE_CMP_EXPR handles ==, !=, <, >, <=, >= and produces 0/1 via x86 setCC + movzx.

assert(x == 42, "test") works. All comparison operators supported in args.

[[entries]]
name = "gvar_overflow"
description = "Global var initializer table limited to 64 entries — use enums for constants"
category = "cyrius"
---
LIMITATION: The gvar_toks array at 0x8FA98 stores saved token indices for
deferred global var initialization. It has 512 bytes = 64 slots.

If a program has >64 global vars with initializers (var x = value),
the array overflows into gvar_cnt at 0x8FC98, causing corruption.

SOLUTION: Use enums instead of vars for constant groups.
Enums have their own init path (PARSE_ENUM_DEF) that doesn't use gvar_toks.

  # BAD — 50 vars = 50 gvar_toks entries:
  var SYS_READ = 0;
  var SYS_WRITE = 1;
  ...50 more...

  # GOOD — 0 gvar_toks entries:
  enum SysNr { SYS_READ = 0; SYS_WRITE = 1; ... }

COUNTING: stdlib uses ~3 globals (alloc), io.cyr uses ~9 (flags),
so programs have ~52 slots remaining. With agnosys using enums,
even large programs stay well under the limit.

PERMANENT FIX: Expand gvar_toks to 256 or 512 entries (change heap map offsets).

[[entries]]
name = "fork_exec_pattern"
description = "How to run subprocesses in Cyrius using fork + execve + waitpid"
category = "cyrius"
---
Cyrius subprocess execution using agnosys syscall wrappers:

BASIC PATTERN (run command, get exit code):
  var pid = sys_fork();
  if (pid == 0) {
      # Child process
      var argv[3];
      store64(&argv, "/usr/bin/cmd");
      store64(&argv + 8, "arg1");
      store64(&argv + 16, 0);  # NULL terminator
      var envp[1];
      store64(&envp, 0);       # NULL envp
      sys_execve("/usr/bin/cmd", &argv, &envp);
      sys_exit(127);            # If exec fails
  }
  # Parent: wait for child
  var status_buf[1];
  sys_waitpid(pid, &status_buf, 0);
  var exit_code = WEXITSTATUS(load32(&status_buf));

CAPTURE OUTPUT PATTERN (run command, read stdout):
  var pipefd[2];
  sys_pipe(&pipefd);
  var read_fd = load64(&pipefd);
  var write_fd = load64(&pipefd + 8);
  var pid = sys_fork();
  if (pid == 0) {
      sys_close(read_fd);
      sys_dup2(write_fd, 1);   # Redirect stdout to pipe
      sys_close(write_fd);
      # ... exec ...
  }
  sys_close(write_fd);
  var n = sys_read(read_fd, buf, buflen);  # Read child output
  sys_close(read_fd);
  sys_waitpid(pid, &status_buf, 0);

COMPILE PATTERN (cyrb uses this):
  # Child: open source as stdin, output as stdout, exec cc2
  sys_dup2(sys_open(source, O_RDONLY, 0), 0);
  sys_dup2(sys_open(output, O_WRONLY|O_CREAT|O_TRUNC, 0x1ED), 1);
  sys_execve("./build/cc2", argv, envp);

KEY GOTCHAS:
- argv must be NULL-terminated array of pointers
- envp can be [NULL] for empty environment
- Child inherits all parent fds — close unused pipe ends
- sys_waitpid(-1, ..., WNOHANG) for non-blocking reap
- WIFEXITED/WEXITSTATUS macros decode the status word

[[entries]]
name = "agnostik_patterns"
description = "Patterns used in agnostik rewrite: error codes, bitmask permissions, heap structs"
category = "cyrius"
---
AGNOSTIK LIBRARY PATTERNS (lib/agnostik/):

ERROR CODES (error.cyr):
  enum AgnostikError { ERR_NONE = 0; ERR_AGENT_NOT_FOUND = 1001; ... }
  fn err_is_retriable(code) { if (code == ERR_TIMEOUT) { return 1; } ... }
  fn err_print(code) { if (code == ERR_NONE) { print("OK"); } ... }
  fn err_fatal(code) { err_eprint(code); syscall(60, 1); }

BITMASK PERMISSIONS (security.cyr):
  enum Permission { PERM_FILE_READ = 1; PERM_NETWORK = 4; PERM_SPAWN = 8; }
  fn sec_has_perm(ctx, perm) { if ((load64(ctx+8) & perm) != 0) { return 1; } }
  fn sec_grant_perm(ctx, perm) { store64(ctx+8, load64(ctx+8) | perm); }
  fn sec_revoke_perm(ctx, perm) { store64(ctx+8, load64(ctx+8) & (0-1-perm)); }

HEAP-ALLOCATED STRUCT (agent.cyr):
  var AGENT_CONFIG_SIZE = 80;
  fn agent_config_new(name) {
      var cfg = alloc(AGENT_CONFIG_SIZE);
      store64(cfg, name);        # offset 0: name pointer
      store64(cfg + 8, AGENT_USER);  # offset 8: type
      store64(cfg + 16, RESTART_NEVER);  # offset 16: restart policy
      ... return cfg;
  }
  fn agent_config_name(cfg) { return load64(cfg); }
  fn agent_config_type(cfg) { return load64(cfg + 8); }
  fn agent_config_set_type(cfg, t) { store64(cfg + 8, t); return 0; }

AUDIT LOGGING (audit.cyr):
  fn audit_print(e) {
      print("["); severity_print(load64(e+24)); print("] agent=");
      fmt_int(load64(e+8)); print(" "); ... print("\n");
  }

PATTERN: Every struct has new/get/set functions. Offsets are manual (8 bytes per field).
PATTERN: Print functions per enum/struct for debugging.
PATTERN: Include order matters — stdlib first, then agnostik modules.

[[entries]]
name = "agnosys_patterns"
description = "Patterns for agnosys syscall bindings: enum constants, sigset, epoll, timerfd"
category = "cyrius"
---
AGNOSYS LIBRARY PATTERNS (lib/agnosys/syscalls.cyr):

SYSCALL CONSTANTS AS ENUMS (avoids gvar_toks overflow):
  enum SysNr { SYS_READ = 0; SYS_WRITE = 1; SYS_OPEN = 2; ... SYS_EPOLL_CREATE1 = 291; }
  enum Signal { SIGHUP = 1; SIGINT = 2; ... SIGPWR = 30; }
  enum MsFlag { MS_RDONLY = 1; MS_NOSUID = 2; MS_NODEV = 4; MS_NOEXEC = 8; }

SYSCALL WRAPPERS:
  fn sys_open(path, flags, mode) { return syscall(SYS_OPEN, path, flags, mode); }
  fn sys_fork() { return syscall(SYS_FORK); }
  fn sys_execve(path, argv, envp) { return syscall(SYS_EXECVE, path, argv, envp); }
  fn sys_mount(source, target, fstype, flags, data) {
      return syscall(SYS_MOUNT, source, target, fstype, flags, data);
  }

SIGNAL SET (64-bit bitmask):
  fn sigset_new() { var set = alloc(8); store64(set, 0); return set; }
  fn sigset_add(set, signum) { store64(set, load64(set) | (1 << (signum-1))); }
  fn sigset_has(set, signum) { return (load64(set) & (1 << (signum-1))) != 0; }

EPOLL EVENT (12-byte struct: u32 events + u64 data):
  fn epoll_event_new(events, data) {
      var ev = alloc(16);
      store32(ev, events);
      store64(ev + 4, data);  # Note: 4-byte offset, not 8
      return ev;
  }

TIMERSPEC (32-byte struct: interval + initial):
  fn timerspec_new(interval_sec, initial_sec) {
      var ts = alloc(32);
      store64(ts, interval_sec); store64(ts+8, 0);   # interval
      store64(ts+16, initial_sec); store64(ts+24, 0); # initial value
      return ts;
  }

WAIT STATUS DECODING:
  fn WIFEXITED(s) { return (s & 0x7F) == 0; }
  fn WEXITSTATUS(s) { return (s >> 8) & 0xFF; }
  fn WIFSIGNALED(s) { var sig = s & 0x7F; return sig > 0 && sig != 0x7F; }
  fn WTERMSIG(s) { return s & 0x7F; }

ERROR CHECKING:
  fn is_err(ret) { return ret < 0; }
  fn err_code(ret) { return 0 - ret; }

[[entries]]
name = "kybernet_patterns"
description = "PID 1 init patterns: console redirect, signal fd, zombie reaping, mount, cgroup"
category = "cyrius"
---
KYBERNET LIBRARY PATTERNS (lib/kybernet/):

CONSOLE REDIRECT (console.cyr):
  Close inherited fds 0/1/2.
  Open /dev/null as stdin (fd 0).
  Open /dev/console as stdout (fd 1), fallback /dev/null.
  dup2(1, 2) for stderr.

SIGNAL HANDLING (signals.cyr):
  Block SIGCHLD/SIGTERM/SIGINT/SIGHUP/SIGPWR via sigprocmask.
  Create signalfd for epoll-based dispatch.
  Read 128-byte signalfd_siginfo struct, signal number at offset 0 (u32).

ZOMBIE REAPING (reaper.cyr):
  Loop: sys_waitpid(-1, &status, WNOHANG) until pid <= 0.
  Decode exit status. Store (pid, code) pairs in vec.
  PID 1 MUST reap all orphans or they become zombies.

PRIVILEGE DROP (privdrop.cyr):
  ORDER MATTERS: setgroups(0, NULL) → setgid(gid) → setuid(uid).
  Can't change groups after setuid drops root.
  Call in child process after fork, before exec.

ESSENTIAL MOUNTS (mount.cyr):
  Check /proc/self/mounts before mounting (avoid EBUSY).
  Mount order: /proc → /sys → /dev/pts → /dev/shm → /run → /sys/fs/cgroup.
  Flags: MS_NOSUID|MS_NODEV|MS_NOEXEC for virtual filesystems.
  Create mount point with sys_mkdir before mount.

CGROUP v2 (cgroup.cyr):
  Path: /sys/fs/cgroup/kybernet.slice/{service}/
  move_to_cgroup: write PID to cgroup.procs file.
  kill_cgroup: write "1" to cgroup.kill (kernel 5.14+), or read PIDs and SIGKILL each.
  All operations are pure filesystem (no special syscalls).

EPOLL EVENT LOOP (eventloop.cyr):
  Create epoll + timerfd for health/watchdog.
  Register signal fd + timers with epoll_add_read.
  Event tokens: TOKEN_SIGNAL=1, TOKEN_HEALTH=2, TOKEN_WATCHDOG=3.
  epoll_wait with EINTR retry.

[[entries]]
name = "nous_ark_patterns"
description = "Resolver and package manager patterns: marketplace resolution, CLI tools, file DB"
category = "cyrius"
---
NOUS RESOLVER (lib/nous/nous.cyr):

SOURCE DETECTION: name contains '/' → marketplace, ends '.flutter' → flutter, else unknown.

MARKETPLACE RESOLUTION:
  Build path: marketplace_dir/name/manifest.json
  Read file, parse JSON manually (find "version": "..." between quotes).
  Return ResolvedPackage with name, version, source, description.

SYSTEM RESOLUTION (apt-based):
  run_capture("/usr/bin/apt-cache", "show", name, buf, 4096)
  Parse "Version: " and "Description:" lines from output.
  Uses fork+exec+pipe pattern from agnosys.

STRATEGY DISPATCH:
  STRAT_MARKETPLACE_FIRST: try marketplace → system
  STRAT_SYSTEM_FIRST: try system → marketplace
  STRAT_ONLY_*: single source

SIMPLE JSON PARSING (no serde, no parser library):
  Scan for "\"key\"" in buffer.
  Find next quote pair after colon.
  Extract bytes between quotes, null-terminate.
  Works for flat JSON objects. Not recursive.

ARK PACKAGE MANAGER (programs/ark.cyr):

CLI DISPATCH:
  args_init() + argc()/argv() for argument parsing.
  streq(cmd, "install") for command matching.
  Each command is a separate function.

FILE-BASED PACKAGE DATABASE:
  Format: name|version|source\n (one entry per line)
  db_load(): read file, split into vec of line strings.
  db_has(): scan vec for "name|" prefix match.
  db_register(): append entry to file (O_APPEND).
  cmd_remove(): rewrite file without matching entry.

TRANSACTION LOG:
  Append-only text file: "action package\n"
  log_transaction("install", name) on every operation.

VERIFICATION:
  Read all entries, check each has ≥2 pipe separators.
  Report corrupt entries.

BINARY SIZE: ark is 44KB, comparable to individual coreutils.

[[entries]]
name = "build_tool_pattern"
description = "cyrb: how to build a Cyrius program that compiles other Cyrius programs"
category = "cyrius"
---
CYRB BUILD TOOL (programs/cyrb.cyr):

COMPILE PATTERN:
  Fork child.
  Child: open source as stdin (dup2), open output as stdout (dup2), exec cc2.
  Parent: waitpid, check exit code, chmod +x output.

  fn compile(source, output) {
      var pid = sys_fork();
      if (pid == 0) {
          sys_dup2(sys_open(source, O_RDONLY, 0), 0);
          sys_dup2(sys_open(output, O_WRONLY|O_CREAT|O_TRUNC, 0x1ED), 1);
          sys_execve(CC2_PATH, argv, envp);
          sys_exit(127);
      }
      sys_waitpid(pid, &status, 0);
      sys_chmod(output, 0x1ED);  # rwxr-xr-x
  }

SELF-HOSTING CHECK:
  cc2 compiles cc2.cyr → cc3. cc3 compiles cc2.cyr → cc4.
  Compare cc3 and cc4 byte-by-byte using heap buffers + memeq.
  NOTE: Use heap (alloc) not stack arrays for compare buffers.
  Large stack arrays (var buf[8192] = 64KB) caused binary data leak.

TEST RUNNER:
  compile(test_source, "/tmp/cyrb_test")
  run_cmd("/tmp/cyrb_test", 0, 0)
  Check exit code 0 = pass.

GOTCHA: When doing byte comparison of binaries, use alloc() for buffers,
not stack-allocated arrays. Stack arrays >8KB can cause issues.

[[entries]]
name = "language_gaps_for_parity"
description = "Language features needed for 1:1 Rust parity, identified during crate rewrites"
category = "cyrius"
---
Features needed for 1:1 Rust ports, identified from agnostik/nous/ark rewrites.
Grouped by blocking priority.

STATUS (as of v1.9.0):
  DONE:
    Enums with data:   enum Result { Ok(val) = 0; Err(code) = 1; } — v1.1.0
    Module system:     mod/pub/use with name mangling — v0.9.7
    HashMap:           lib/hashmap.cyr (string→i64) — v0.9.12
    Pattern matching:  match expr { val => { } _ => { } } — v0.9.8
    Closures:          |x| expr, |a, b| a + b — v0.9.10
    Conditional comp:  #ifdef/#define/#endif, cyrb -D flag — v0.9.6/v1.6.2
    Traits:            impl Type for Trait { fn method(self) {} } — v0.9.9

  REMAINING:
    Generics:          Syntax parsed (fn f<T>()) but not enforced. Monomorphize manually.
    String formatting: sprintf from lib/fmt.cyr, not format!() macro
    Serde:             Manual JSON via lib/json.cyr. No derive macros.
    Derive macros:     Not implemented. Manual accessor functions.
    Lifetimes:         Not implemented. No borrow checker.

CURRENT PATTERNS:
  Rust Result<T>     → packed Result: Ok(v) returns v, Err(v) sets bit 63. Zero alloc.
  Rust Option<T>     → heap tagged union: Some(v)/None() via lib/tagged.cyr
  Rust String        → Str struct (data+len) via lib/str.cyr, or C strings
  Rust HashMap       → lib/hashmap.cyr (string keys, i64 values)
  Rust traits        → impl blocks with name mangling (Type_method)
  Rust generics      → everything is i64 (Vec is always Vec<i64>)
  Rust serde JSON    → lib/json.cyr (flat key-value, manual nested parse)

These workarounds work for system code but don't scale for application code.
Phase 13 (enums-with-data, generics, traits, modules) is the path to 1:1 parity.

[[entries]]
name = "hashmap"
description = "hashmap.cyr: FNV-1a hash table with string keys and i64 values"
category = "cyrius"
---
HASHMAP (lib/hashmap.cyr):

Open addressing with linear probing. Auto-grows at 70% load.
Keys are null-terminated strings. Values are i64.

  var m = map_new();
  map_set(m, "name", 42);
  var v = map_get(m, "name");       # 42
  map_has(m, "name");               # 1
  map_delete(m, "name");
  var keys = map_keys(m);           # vec of string pointers
  map_count(m);                     # number of entries
  map_print(m);                     # {key: val, ...}

IMPLEMENTATION:
  Entry = { key_ptr, value, occupied } = 24 bytes
  Header = { entries_ptr, capacity, count } = 24 bytes
  Hash: FNV-1a (0xCBF29CE484222325 seed, 0x100000001B3 prime)
  Growth: double capacity when count * 10 >= capacity * 7
  Capacity must be power of 2 (for & mask instead of %)

GOTCHA: Values are always i64. String pointers are i64.
If you store a string pointer, map_print shows the raw number.

[[entries]]
name = "string_enhancements"
description = "str.cyr extended: contains, split, join, trim, builder, from_int, to_int"
category = "cyrius"
---
STRING ENHANCEMENTS (lib/str.cyr):

New functions added to the Str (fat pointer) type:

  str_contains(s, "needle")    — substring search, returns 0/1
  str_starts_with(s, "pre")    — prefix check
  str_ends_with(s, "suf")      — suffix check
  str_index_of(s, byte)        — find byte, returns index or -1
  str_from_int(42)              — integer to Str ("42")
  str_to_int(s)                 — parse Str to integer
  str_trim(s)                   — strip leading/trailing whitespace
  str_split(s, byte_sep)        — split into vec of Str
  str_join(parts_vec, ",")      — join vec of Str with separator
  str_clone(s)                  — deep copy (new buffer)

STRING BUILDER:
  var sb = str_builder_new();
  str_builder_add_cstr(sb, "hello ");
  str_builder_add_int(sb, 42);
  var result = str_builder_build(sb);  # "hello 42"

PATTERN: Use str_builder for concatenating many parts.
Direct str_cat is O(n) per call. Builder is O(total) once.

[[entries]]
name = "traits"
description = "trait.cyr: vtable-based trait objects for polymorphic dispatch (Display, Eq, From)"
category = "cyrius"
---
TRAITS (lib/trait.cyr):

Traits are vtables — heap-allocated arrays of function pointers.
A "trait object" is a fat pointer: { vtable_ptr, data_ptr } = 16 bytes.

DEFINING A TRAIT (Display example):
  Slot 0: print(data)       — print to stdout
  Slot 1: to_string(data)   — return Str representation

  var vt = display_vtable(&my_print, &my_to_string);
  var obj = trait_obj_new(vt, my_data);
  display(obj);              — calls my_print(my_data)
  var s = to_string(obj);    — calls my_to_string(my_data)

IMPLEMENTING FOR A TYPE:
  fn _point_print(p) { fmt_int(load64(p)); ... }
  fn _point_to_string(p) { ... return str; }
  var _point_vt = 0;
  fn point_as_display(p) {
      if (_point_vt == 0) { _point_vt = display_vtable(&_point_print, &_point_to_string); }
      return trait_obj_new(_point_vt, p);
  }

POLYMORPHISM:
  var items = vec_new();
  vec_push(items, int_as_display(1));
  vec_push(items, str_as_display(str_from("two")));
  vec_push(items, point_as_display(&p));
  # All callable via display(item) — dispatches through vtable

BUILT-IN IMPLEMENTATIONS:
  int_as_display(n)   — wraps i64 as Display
  str_as_display(s)   — wraps Str as Display

AVAILABLE TRAITS:
  Display (print + to_string), Eq (equality), From (conversion), Default

PATTERN: Lazy vtable init — create vtable on first use, cache in global var.

[[entries]]
name = "generics_syntax"
description = "Generic syntax fn foo<T>() and struct Bar<T> parsed — no codegen change (everything is i64)"
category = "cyrius"
---
GENERICS (Phase 1 — syntax only):

The compiler now accepts generic parameter syntax:
  fn identity<T>(x: T) { return x; }
  fn map<K, V>(key: K, val: V) { ... }
  struct Pair<T> { first; second; }
  struct Result<T, E> { ok; err; }
  var x: Vec<i64> = vec_new();
  fn f<T>(v: Vec<T>) { ... }

WHAT HAPPENS: The compiler parses <...> after function/struct names
and in type annotations, then IGNORES them. Everything is still i64.
No monomorphization. No type checking. No code duplication.

WHY THIS WORKS: In Cyrius, all values are 8 bytes (i64). There is no
runtime difference between Vec<i64> and Vec<String> — both are the
same code, same layout, same machine instructions. Generics are purely
documentation and future-proofing.

COMPILER CHANGES:
  1. SKIP_GENERICS(S) — helper in parse.cyr: if token is <, skip to >
  2. Called after fn name, struct name, and in type annotation positions
  3. Also added to cc2.cyr pass 1 fn-skip and pass 2 struct-skip
  4. Synced to cc2_aarch64.cyr

PHASE 2 (future): Type parameter tracking, mismatch warnings.

[[entries]]
name = "preprocessor_include_bug"
description = "CRITICAL: preprocessor eats string literals containing 'include quote' pattern"
category = "cyrius"
---
BUG: The preprocessor (PREPROCESS in lex.cyr) scans raw source bytes for the
pattern: i-n-c-l-u-d-e-space-quote. It does NOT distinguish between code and
string literals.

If a string literal contains this pattern, the preprocessor will try to
process it as a file inclusion, corrupting the source.

EXAMPLE (BREAKS):
  if (memeq(buf, "include \"", 9) == 1) { ... }
  # Preprocessor sees 'include "' and tries to open file '", 9) == 1) {...}'

WORKAROUND: Build the pattern at runtime using byte stores:
  var _pat[2];
  fn _init_pat() {
      store8(&_pat, 105);   # i
      store8(&_pat + 1, 110); # n
      ... etc ...
      store8(&_pat + 8, 34);  # "
      return 0;
  }
  # Then use: memeq(buf, &_pat, 9)

PROPER FIX: Modify PREPROCESS to track whether it is inside a string literal
(between unescaped quotes). Only trigger include processing when NOT inside
a string.

**Status (2026-05-03)**: ✅ **FIXED in cyrius v5.8.40** (shipped
2026-05-03). PP_PASS + PP_IFDEF_PASS in `src/frontend/lex_pp.cyr`
now track `"`-bounded string-literal state via `in_string` +
`escape_next` flags; the `if (bol == 1)` directive-detection block
is gated on `&& in_string == 0`, mirroring the v5.7.36 cyrlint
string-literal awareness fix shape. cc5 +640 B (320 B per pass).
Workaround above remains valid for **pre-v5.8.40 toolchains**;
new code on cyrius >= 5.8.40 can use the literal pattern directly.
Tracked at: `cyrius/docs/development/issues/2026-05-01-preprocessor-
include-pattern-in-string-literals.md` (issue file kept for
historical reference + the workaround text).

This affects any program that needs to scan source files for include
directives (e.g., cyrc vet/deny). Also affects comments containing
the word "include" followed by a quote — be careful in documentation.

[[entries]]
name = "cyrc_tool"
description = "cyrc: dependency auditing (vet) and policy enforcement (deny) for Cyrius projects"
category = "cyrius"
---
CYRC — CYRIUS CHECK (programs/cyrc.cyr, 22KB binary):

cyrc vet <file>:
  Scans source file for include directives.
  Reports each dependency as OK, UNTRUST, or MISSING.
  Trusted paths: lib/, src/frontend/, src/backend/, kernel/
  Returns 0 if all trusted and present, 1 otherwise.

cyrc deny <file>:
  Enforces project policies:
  - No absolute path includes (/foo/bar.cyr)
  - No parent directory traversal (../something.cyr)
  - Source file must exist
  Returns 0 if no violations, count of violations otherwise.

SEPARATE FROM CYRB: cyrc is a separate binary, not part of cyrb.
Reason: combining too many functions in one program hits compiler
limits (large programs with many includes exceed fixup table or
code buffer capacity). Keeping tools focused and small is better.

GOTCHA: Cannot use "include \"" as a string literal in the source
due to the preprocessor bug. Pattern must be built at runtime.

[[entries]]
name = "tagged_unions"
description = "tagged.cyr: Option, Result, Either types via heap-allocated tag+value pairs"
category = "cyrius"
---
TAGGED UNIONS (lib/tagged.cyr):

A tagged union is a heap-allocated pair: { tag: i64, value: i64 } = 16 bytes.
tag(t) returns the discriminant. payload(t) returns the value.

OPTION TYPE:
  var x = Some(42);      # tag=1, value=42
  var y = None();         # tag=0, value=0
  if (is_some(x) == 1) { var v = unwrap(x); }
  var v = unwrap_or(y, 99);  # returns 99 (fallback)
  # unwrap() aborts on None with error message

RESULT TYPE:
  fn divide(a, b) {
      if (b == 0) { return Err(1); }
      return Ok(a / b);
  }
  var r = divide(42, 2);
  if (is_ok(r) == 1) { var v = result_unwrap(r); }  # v = 21
  # result_unwrap() aborts on Err with "result_unwrap on Err(N)"

EITHER TYPE:
  var l = Left(10);
  var r = Right(20);
  if (is_left(l) == 1) { ... }

PRINT HELPERS:
  option_print(x) → "Some(42)" or "None"
  result_print(r) → "Ok(21)" or "Err(1)"

GOTCHA: Do not use "default" as a parameter name — it is a reserved keyword.
Use "fallback" instead (e.g., unwrap_or(opt, fallback)).

IMPLEMENTATION: No compiler changes. Pure library using alloc(16) + store64.
Compiler syntax (enum Foo { None; Some(val); }) deferred — library works well.

REAL-WORLD PATTERNS:
  # Safe file read
  fn safe_read(path, buf, len) {
      var fd = file_open(path, 0, 0);
      if (fd < 0) { return Err(0 - fd); }
      var n = file_read(fd, buf, len);
      file_close(fd);
      return Ok(n);
  }

[[entries]]
name = "aarch64_codegen_fixes"
description = "All aarch64 codegen bugs found and fixed — instruction encoding, register ordering, ABI"
category = "cyrius"
---
AARCH64 CODEGEN FIXES (cumulative):

1. INITIAL BRANCH: cc2_aarch64.cyr emitted x86 JMP (0xE9) instead of aarch64 B.
   Fix: Replace EB(S, 0xE9)+4 placeholders with EJMP0(S) from jump.cyr.

2. RECFIX ORDERING: RECFIX recorded CP after MOVZ, but FIXUP_MOV expected CP of MOVZ.
   Fix: Call RECFIX BEFORE the first EW in EVSTORE/EVLOAD/EVADDR.

3. POP ENCODING: EPOPR/EPOPC used pre-indexed load (0xF8410FE0 = [sp, #16]!)
   instead of post-indexed (0xF84107E0 = [sp], #16). Loaded from wrong address.
   Fix: Change bit [11] from 1→0: 0xF8410FE0 → 0xF84107E0.

4. MODULO: ECQO was no-op, EIDIV overwrote dividend, EMOVDR returned unset x2.
   Fix: ECQO saves dividend (mov x2, x0), EIDIV divides saved value (sdiv x0, x2, x1),
   EMOVDR computes remainder (msub x0, x0, x1, x2). Note: msub encoding 0x9B018800
   not 0x9B018840 (Rn must be x0, not x2).

5. ARCH-SPECIFIC FUNCTIONS IN PARSE.CYR: 14 functions (EFNPRO, ESUBRSP, EFNEPI,
   ECALLTO, ECALLFIX, EPOPARG, ECALLPOPS, ECALLCLEAN, EDISP32, ESTOREREGPARM,
   ESTORESTACKPARM, ESTOREPARM, EFLLOAD, EFLSTORE) were hardcoded x86 in parse.cyr.
   Fix: Moved to cc/emit.cyr (x86) and arch/aarch64/emit.cyr (aarch64).

6. STRUCT FIELD ACCESS: EMIT_STRUCT_FIELD, PARSE_FIELD_LOAD, PARSE_FIELD_STORE
   used inline x86 (mov rcx + add rcx). Fix: Refactored to EVADDR_X1 + EADDIMM_X1
   (arch-independent helpers defined in each emit file).

7. FUNCTION ABI: aarch64 uses STP x29,x30,[sp,#-16]! for prologue,
   LDP x29,x30,[sp],#16 for epilogue. Locals via STUR/LDUR [x29, #-offset].
   Calls via BL. Parameters in x0-x7 (vs x86 rdi,rsi,rdx,rcx,r8,r9).

8. ENUM INIT + SCANNING: cc2_aarch64.cyr was missing enum handling in pass 1/2
   and the init ordering fix. Synced from cc2.cyr.

RESULT: 29/29 aarch64 tests passing (arithmetic, control flow, functions,
structs, enums, strings, syscalls, load/store).

[[entries]]
name = "string_formatting"
description = "fmt_sprintf: printf-like string formatting with %d, %x, %s, %% into a buffer"
category = "cyrius"
---
STRING FORMATTING (lib/fmt.cyr):

fmt_sprintf(buf, format, args) — write formatted string into buf, return length.
args is a vec of i64 values consumed in order.

  Format specifiers:
    %d — decimal integer (fmt_int_buf)
    %x — hex integer (fmt_hex_buf)
    %s — null-terminated string (memcpy)
    %% — literal % character

  Usage:
    var buf[128];
    var args = vec_new();
    vec_push(args, "Cyrius");
    vec_push(args, 168);
    vec_push(args, 0);
    fmt_sprintf(&buf, "%s: %d tests, %d failures", args);
    println(&buf);
    # Output: Cyrius: 168 tests, 0 failures

  fmt_printf(format, args) — format + print to stdout.

GOTCHA: Use unique var names in each specifier branch (dval/dtmp/dlen for %d,
xval/xtmp/xlen for %x) because Cyrius has no block scoping.

Also added: fmt_hex_buf(n, buf) — hex number to buffer, returns length.

[[entries]]
name = "bounds_checking"
description = "bounds.cyr: opt-in runtime bounds checking for arrays and buffers"
category = "cyrius"
---
BOUNDS CHECKING (lib/bounds.cyr):

Opt-in checked memory access. Aborts with error message on violation.

  checked_load64(buf, len, idx)   — load 64-bit slot, abort if idx >= len or < 0
  checked_store64(buf, len, idx, val) — store, same bounds check
  checked_load8(buf, len, idx)    — byte load with bounds check
  checked_store8(buf, len, idx, val) — byte store with bounds check
  checked_memcpy(dst, dst_len, src, src_len, count) — abort if count exceeds either

Error output goes to stderr with index and length info:
  "bounds: index 10 >= len 4"

Uses fmt_int_buf + syscall(1, 2, ...) to write to stderr without touching stdout.
Process exits with code 1 on violation.

PATTERN: Use checked_ functions during development, switch to raw load/store
for hot paths after correctness is proven.

NOTE: vec.cyr already has bounds checking in vec_get/vec_set (exits on violation).
bounds.cyr adds the same for raw array buffers.

[[entries]]
name = "nested_calls_work"
description = "Nested function calls f(g(x)) already work — no fix needed"
category = "cyrius"
---
NESTED FUNCTION CALLS — NOT BROKEN (verified 2026-04-05):

Tested cases that all pass:
  double(add1(20)) = 42          # nested as argument
  f(g(10), g(11)) = 42           # nested in multi-arg
  f(f(f(42))) = 42               # triple nesting
  f(20) + f(22) = 42             # calls in arithmetic
  1 + f(41) = 42                 # call in right operand
  f(20) * 2 = 42                 # call in left operand

WHY IT WORKS: PARSE_FNCALL pushes each argument via EPUSHR after evaluation.
Inner function calls happen during argument evaluation, but their result
goes into rax, which then gets pushed. The call/return convention preserves
the stack correctly because each call saves rbp and has its own frame.

This was listed as a Phase 13 item but turns out to already work.
No compiler change needed.

[[entries]]
name = "cyrb_dispatcher"
description = "cyrb is a shell dispatcher with dependency management, cross-compilation, and pulsar rebuild"
category = "cyrius"
---
CYRB DISPATCHER (scripts/cyrb):

cyrb is a shell script that dispatches subcommands to individual tools.

COMMANDS (v1.9.2):
  Build:    cyrb build [--aarch64 [--native]] [-D NAME]... <src> <out>
            cyrb run [--aarch64 [--native]] [-D NAME]... <src>
            cyrb test [--aarch64 [--native]] [-D NAME]... <test.cyr>
            cyrb bench <bench.cyr>
            cyrb check <source.cyr>
            cyrb self — self-hosting check
  Project:  cyrb init, package, publish, install, update
            cyrb deps — show resolved dependency tree
  Quality:  cyrb audit, fmt, lint, doc, vet, deny
  Rebuild:  cyrb pulsar — rebuild cc2 + cc2_aarch64 + native + tools from source, install
  Info:     cyrb version, which, help

THREE COMPILERS:
  cc2                  — x86→x86 (native, ~176KB)
  cc2_aarch64          — x86→aarch64 (cross-compiler, ~163KB)
  cc2-native-aarch64   — aarch64→aarch64 (native ARM, ~209KB)
  --aarch64            uses cross-compiler
  --aarch64 --native   uses native compiler (runs under qemu on x86)

DEPENDENCY RESOLUTION (v1.9.1):
  cyrb build reads [deps] from cyrb.toml and auto-resolves includes.
  See "dependency_management" entry for details.

COMPILE PIPELINE:
  1. Read [deps] from cyrb.toml → resolve includes
  2. Prepend #define lines from -D flags
  3. Prepend resolved includes
  4. Cat source file
  5. Pipe to compiler → output binary

PULSAR (v1.9.0):
  cyrb pulsar rebuilds the entire toolchain from source in ~410ms:
  1. Compile cc2 from src/main.cyr (with self-host verification)
  2. Cross-compile cc2_aarch64 + self-host to native aarch64
  3. Rebuild tools (cyrfmt, cyrlint, cyrdoc, cyrc, ark)
  4. Install to ~/.cyrius/versions/<VER>/, purge old versions
  5. Verify installed compiler works
  Auto two-step bootstrap when heap layout changes.

[[entries]]
name = "dependency_management"
description = "cyrb.toml [deps] section: stdlib, path, and git dependencies with module filtering (v1.9.1)"
category = "cyrius"
---
DEPENDENCY FORMAT (cyrb.toml):

  [deps]
  stdlib = ["string", "fmt", "alloc", "vec", "str", "hashmap"]

  [deps.agnosys]
  path = "../agnosys"
  modules = ["lib/syscalls_linux.cyr", "src/error.cyr"]

  [deps.kybernet]
  git = "https://github.com/MacCracken/kybernet"
  tag = "0.3.0"
  modules = ["lib/net.cyr"]

THREE DEPENDENCY TYPES:
  1. stdlib — resolved from installed Cyrius (~/.cyrius/versions/<ver>/lib/)
     or local lib/ (dev mode). Just list module names without .cyr extension.
  2. path — local project on disk. Absolute or relative to project root.
  3. git — remote repository. Cloned to ~/.cyrius/deps/<name>/<tag>/.
     Supports GitHub, GitLab, Codeberg, any git URL.
     tag= pins to a specific release/tag. Defaults to "main" if omitted.

MODULE FILTERING (v1.9.2):
  modules = ["lib/syscalls.cyr", "src/error.cyr"]
  If specified, only listed files are included from the dependency.
  If omitted, ALL lib/*.cyr + src/*.cyr (except main.cyr) are included.
  Prevents pulling in unused modules from large dependencies.

RESOLUTION ORDER:
  1. All stdlib modules (in order listed)
  2. All path/git deps (in order listed in manifest)
  3. Source entry file (src/main.cyr or whatever entry= specifies)

INCLUDE-ONCE:
  Duplicate includes are silently skipped (v1.8.0 include-once semantics).
  If stdlib and a dep both provide string.cyr, only the first is processed.

COMMANDS:
  cyrb deps — show resolved dependency tree and include list
  cyrb build — auto-resolves deps before compilation
  cyrb update — syncs lib/ from installed stdlib (for vendored projects)

[[entries]]
name = "package_format"
description = "Three-layer package system: cyrb.toml (manifest), .ark (distributable), zugot (recipe)"
category = "cyrius"
---
CYRIUS PACKAGE SYSTEM:

Three layers, matching how Rust has Cargo.toml / crates.io / system packages:

LAYER 1 — cyrb.toml (project manifest, lives in project repo):
  [package]
  name = "kybernet"
  version = "0.9.0"
  description = "PID 1 init system"
  license = "GPL-3.0-only"
  language = "cyrius"

  [build]
  entry = "src/main.cyr"
  test = "src/test.cyr"
  output = "kybernet"

LAYER 2 — .ark package (created by cyrb package):
  kybernet-0.9.0.ark (gzipped tar):
    manifest.json — {"name", "version", "description", "license", "type"}
    kybernet      — compiled binary

  cyrb package reads cyrb.toml, compiles entry, creates .ark
  cyrb publish uploads .ark to Ark marketplace (future)

LAYER 3 — zugot recipe (lives in zugot repo, for system builds):
  zugot/base/kybernet.toml — TOML recipe with:
    [package] metadata
    [source] github_release + sha256
    [depends] runtime=[], build=["cyrius"]
    [build] make = "cat src/main.cyr | cc2 > build/kybernet"
    [security] hardening flags

  Used by takumi (build system) for AGNOS system images.
  Users install via: ark install kybernet

WORKFLOW:
  Developer: cyrb package → .ark → cyrb publish → Ark registry
  System:    zugot recipe → takumi build → AGNOS ISO
  User:      ark install kybernet → prebuilt binary from registry

[[entries]]
name = "installer"
description = "install.sh + cyrius version manager — like rustup for Cyrius"
category = "cyrius"
---
STATUS: the SHAPE is current, the NAMES are v0.9/v1.x-era. Read
`cc2` as `cycc` (cc2 -> cc3 -> cc5 -> cycc, final since v6.0.0),
`cyrb` as `cyrius`, and `cyrius` (version mgr) as `cyriusly`
(renamed from cyriusup at v3.4.15). Version strings below are
illustrative — current is 6.5.10.

INSTALLER (scripts/install.sh):

  curl -sSf https://raw.githubusercontent.com/.../install.sh | sh

Installs to ~/.cyrius/:
  bin/         cc2, cyrb, ark, cyrc, cyrius (version mgr) — on PATH
  versions/    0.9.0/, 0.10.0/ etc — one dir per version
  current      file containing active version string

INSTALL FLOW:
  1. Detect arch (x86_64 / aarch64)
  2. Try downloading prebuilt binaries from GitHub Releases
  3. Fallback: clone repo + bootstrap from source (40ms)
  4. Symlink active version to bin/
  5. Add ~/.cyrius/bin to PATH (zshrc/bashrc/profile)

VERSION MANAGER (cyrius command):
  cyrius version      — show active (reads ~/.cyrius/current)
  cyrius list         — show all installed
  cyrius use 0.10.0   — switch active (repoints symlinks)
  cyrius install 0.10.0 — install a new version
  cyrius which        — show path to active cc2

PATTERN: Like rustup — installer bootstraps, version manager switches.
Multiple versions coexist. Symlinks point active version to bin/.

[[entries]]
name = "standalone_project"
description = "Pattern for standalone Cyrius projects: vendored stdlib in lib/, no path deps"
category = "cyrius"
---
STATUS: v1.x-era names. `cc2` is `cycc` today, `cyrb` is `cyrius`,
`cyrb.toml` is `cyrius.cyml`, and vendoring is `cyrius deps` (never
a symlink to another repo's lib/ — that pattern wrote through and
corrupted the upstream stdlib four times in v5.5.30-.33). The
`cat src/main.cyr | cc2` line below is NOT how you build a project
on any current toolchain: use `cyrius build`, which resolves deps,
prepends the manifest's includes, and handles cross-arch + strict
flags. Raw `cat | cycc` is compiler-internal self-host only.

STANDALONE PROJECT PATTERN:

Cyrius projects vendor their stdlib in lib/ so they build without
a relative path to the cyrius repo. Only the cc2 binary is needed.

PROJECT STRUCTURE (created by cyrb init):
  myproject/
  ├── cyrb.toml          project manifest
  ├── VERSION
  ├── lib/               vendored Cyrius stdlib
  │   ├── string.cyr
  │   ├── alloc.cyr
  │   ├── agnosys/syscalls.cyr
  │   └── ... (14 modules)
  ├── src/
  │   ├── main.cyr       entry (includes from lib/)
  │   ├── test.cyr
  │   └── lib/            project-specific modules
  ├── scripts/
  │   ├── build.sh
  │   └── test.sh
  └── build/              gitignored

INCLUDE PATHS: All relative to project root:
  include "lib/string.cyr"      ← vendored stdlib
  include "src/lib/module.cyr"  ← project modules

BUILD: cd $ROOT && cat src/main.cyr | cc2 > build/$NAME

VENDORING: cyrb init copies stdlib from the cyrius install.
To update: re-run cyrb init or manually copy from cyrius repo.

WHY VENDOR: Avoids ../cyrius/ path dependency. Project is
self-contained. CI just needs the cc2 binary, not the full repo.

[[entries]]
name = "bench_library"
description = "bench.cyr: nanosecond-precision benchmarking with min/max/avg and human-readable output"
category = "cyrius"
---
BENCHMARKING (lib/bench.cyr):

  var b = bench_new("my_operation");
  bench_run(b, &work_fn, 10000);  # run 10000 iterations
  bench_report(b);
  # Output: my_operation: 407ns avg (min=390ns max=8us) [10000 iters]

API:
  bench_new(name)              — create benchmark
  bench_start(b) / bench_stop(b) — manual timing
  bench_run(b, fp, n)         — auto-run function pointer N times
  bench_report(b)             — print one benchmark
  bench_report_all(benches)   — print vec of benchmarks

  bench_avg_ns(b), bench_min_ns(b), bench_max_ns(b), bench_iterations(b)

TIMING: Uses clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW) via syscall 228.
Resolution is nanoseconds. Output auto-scales: ns → us → ms → s.

PATTERN: Define work functions, create bench, run, report:
  fn work_loop() { var s = 0; for (var i = 0; i < 1000; i = i + 1) { s = s + i; } return 0; }
  var b = bench_new("loop_1000");
  bench_run(b, &work_loop, 1000);

GOTCHA: _fmt_time uses shared var names (major, minor, tlen) — no
block scoping means all vars must be declared at function top.

[[entries]]
name = "callback_library"
description = "callback.cyr: closure-like patterns via function pointers (map, filter, fold, pre_exec)"
category = "cyrius"
---
CALLBACK LIBRARY (lib/callback.cyr):

Replaces closures with function pointer patterns:

  for_each(vec, &fn)         — apply fn(item) to each element
  vec_filter(vec, &fn)       — return new vec where fn(item)==1
  vec_map(vec, &fn)          — return new vec with fn(item) applied
  vec_fold(vec, init, &fn)   — accumulate with fn(acc, item)
  vec_any(vec, &fn)          — return 1 if any fn(item)==1
  vec_all(vec, &fn)          — return 1 if all fn(item)==1
  vec_find_by(vec, &fn)      — return first item where fn(item)==1

  fork_with_pre_exec(cmd, argv, envp, &cb, cb_data)
    — fork, run callback in child before exec (replaces Rust closures in Command::pre_exec)

USAGE:
  fn double(x) { return x * 2; }
  var mapped = vec_map(my_vec, &double);

  fn is_even(x) { if (x % 2 == 0) { return 1; } return 0; }
  var evens = vec_filter(my_vec, &is_even);

LIMITATION: Functions used as callbacks must be named top-level functions.
No anonymous closures. No captured variables (use globals or pass via data pointer).

[[entries]]
name = "v2_release"
description = "v2.0.0 released 2026-04-08. 202KB compiler, multi-width types, unions, bitfields."
category = "cyrius"
---
CYRIUS v2.0.0 — Released 2026-04-08.

COMPILER: 202KB (x86). 9 .tcyr test files (107 assertions). 28 stdlib modules.
BINARY: Self-hosting byte-identical. Heap audit clean (27 regions, 0 overlaps).

v2.0 FEATURES:
  Language: multi-width types (i8/i16/i32/u128), sizeof(Type), unions,
    bitfield builtins (bitget/bitset/bitclr), struct field widths,
    expression type propagation + narrowing warnings.
  Compiler: heap map audit tool, 6 overflow guards, DCE bitmap O(T+N),
    struct field limit 16→32, output buffer relocated (256KB).
  Tooling: .tcyr/.bcyr test format, cyrb build timing, cyrb test --verbose
    --filter, cyrb audit rewritten (5-stage).
  Research: cyrius-x bytecode design (v2.1), multi-file compilation design.

v1.0→v2.0 HISTORY:
  v1.0 (2026-04-06): 128KB, 253 tests, 21 modules.
  v1.11.x: Bug #14 fix (heap collision), inline (added/disabled), R12 spill
    (added/reverted), threads/channels/async, freelist allocator.
  v1.12.x: Heap hardening, struct expansion, output buffer relocation.
  v2.0.0: Type system expansion, unions, bitfields, new test suite.

ECOSYSTEM:
  Argonaut: 424 tests pass. Majra port in progress.
  Sakshi (logging): in development, needs defmt (v2.1 target).
  108 Rust repos (~1M lines) to convert. 5 done + argonaut.

[[entries]]
name = "repo_cleanup"
description = "v1.0 cleanup: cyrius-ref removed, syscalls promoted to stdlib"
category = "cyrius"
---
lib/cyrius-ref/ REMOVED — agnostik, agnosys, kybernet, nous live in own repos.
lib/syscalls.cyr PROMOTED — was agnosys/syscalls.cyr, now core stdlib module.
  50 syscall numbers + 20 wrappers (fork, execve, pipe, mount, epoll, etc.)
  Used by ark, cyrb, and any program needing OS-level calls.

Test programs removed: agnostik_test, kybernet_test, nous_test.
  Each project owns its own test suite now.

kernel/agnos.cyr synced to github.com/MacCracken/agnos (source of truth).

[[entries]]
name = "agnos_kernel_architecture"
description = "AGNOS kernel: 33KB, boots on qemu, architecture and what's needed for shell"
category = "cyrius"
---
AGNOS KERNEL — 33KB, boots on qemu-system-x86_64

DONE:
  Serial I/O, GDT, IDT (256 vectors), PIC, PIT timer, PS/2 keyboard
  Page tables (16MB, 2MB pages), PMM (bitmap, 4096 pages), VMM
  Process table (16 slots), ELF loader, round-robin scheduler
  Syscalls: exit(0), write(1), getpid(2)

NEEDED FOR SHELL:
  1. ramfs (CPIO initrd) — in-memory filesystem
  2. Context switch — save/restore registers on timer IRQ
  3. User/kernel mode — Ring 3 + TSS
  4. Shell — readline, fork+exec, wait
  5. Init (kybernet) — PID 1

SIZE: 17.5KB code + 13KB data (IDT 4KB, PMM 4KB, KB buf 2KB)
  Optimized from 62KB → 33KB by right-sizing IDT (4096→512) and proc_table (512→128).

BOOT: qemu-system-x86_64 -kernel build/agnos -serial stdio -display none
REPO: github.com/MacCracken/agnos (source of truth)

[[entries]]
name = "porting_tool"
description = "cyrb port: one-command Rust→Cyrius project scaffolding"
category = "cyrius"
---
USAGE: cyrb port /path/to/rust-project

WHAT IT DOES:
  1. Moves Rust code to rust-old/ (preserves everything)
  2. Records LINES_OF_RUST.txt
  3. Creates src/, lib/, programs/, tests/ directories
  4. Vendors stdlib from installed Cyrius (~/.cyrius/versions/X/lib/)
  5. Generates src/main.cyr skeleton with common includes
  6. Generates cyrb.toml project manifest
  7. Generates tests/test.sh

PATTERN MAPPING (Rust → Cyrius):
  fn foo(x: i64) -> i64    →  fn foo(x) { return ...; }
  struct Point { x: f64 }  →  struct Point { x; y; }
  impl Point { fn sum() }  →  impl Math for Point { fn sum(self) { } }
  point.sum()               →  point.sum() (same!)
  match result { Ok(v) }   →  if (is_ok(result) == 1) { var v = payload(result); }
  for i in 0..10            →  for i in 0..10 (same!)
  for item in vec           →  for item in vec (same!)
  let f = |x| x * 2        →  var f = |x| x * 2;
  Vec::new()                →  vec_new()
  String::from("x")        →  str_from("x")
  HashMap::new()            →  map_new()
  #[cfg(feature = "x")]     →  #ifdef X

TESTED ON: vidhana (228 lines Rust → compiles and runs as Cyrius skeleton)

[[entries]]
name = "migration_status"
description = "Migration status: 107 repos, ~980K lines, wave plan"
category = "cyrius"
---
TOTAL: 108 Rust repos, ~1M lines
CONVERTED: 5 (agnostik, agnosys, kybernet, nous, ark) + agnosys full port (20 modules, 29K Rust → Cyrius)
REMAINING: 103

ARGONAUT STATUS (v1.11.4+):
  Unblocked. 300 tests + 39 serde + 18 benchmarks all pass.
  Bug #14 (SIGSEGV on 6000+ line programs) fixed — heap offset collision
  between &&/|| extra_patches and continue forward-patches.
  ServiceDefinition has 21 fields (exceeds 16-field struct limit) but
  works due to benign overflow into reinitialized state.

WAVES:
  1. <1K lines (18 repos) — ready now, all patterns covered
  2. 1-5K lines (21 repos) — ready now, need serde=json.cyr
  3. Keystones: hisab (32K) + bhava (30K) — need const generics, derive
  4. Hisab dependents (37 repos) — after wave 3
  5. Security stack (~80K) — needs ownership/borrow checker
  6. AI/platform (~350K) — needs concurrency

KEY INSIGHT: 37 repos depend on hisab. Porting hisab unlocks a third of the ecosystem.

TOOLS: cyrius port (scaffold), cyrius header (C FFI), exec_vec (subprocess bridge)
DOCS: docs/development/migration-strategy.md

[[entries]]
name = "v2x_ecosystem_status"
description = "v2.6: Ecosystem status — stdlib hardened, downstream CI unified, test coverage expanded"
category = "cyrius"
---
COMPILER: v2.6.2, 215KB, self-hosting on x86_64 + aarch64.
STDLIB: 31 modules. 21 test suites, 251 assertions. 5 fuzz harnesses. 9 benchmarks.

DOWNSTREAM CI UNIFIED:
  All downstream repos pin CYRIUS_VERSION in CI/release workflows.
  Pattern: curl tarball from GitHub release → extract → GITHUB_PATH.
  Repos on 2.6.2: majra, sakshi, argonaut, bsp, cyrius-doom.
  LESSON: Pin to latest RELEASED tag, not dev version — CI downloads fail
  if the version isn't tagged yet (GitHub returns HTML 404, gzip fails).

STDLIB BUGS FIXED (v2.6.1):
  math, matrix, regex — all three no longer crash. Root cause was FINDVAR
  bug fixed in v2.1.0, just never retested. str_replace had Str/cstring API
  mismatch (strlen on Str objects).

MAJRA SHA-1 FIX (v2.6.3):
  sha1() in ws.cyr had var w[80] for 640 bytes of data. Massive stack
  corruption caused garbled output. Fixed to heap-allocated alloc(640).
  Test expectation was also wrong (RFC example for different key).

TOOL RENAME (v2.3.2): cyrb → cyrius. cyriusup for version management.
GLOBALS LIMIT (v2.4.0): 256→1024 initialized globals. Unblocked agnosys.

[[entries]]
name = "v3_ecosystem_status"
description = "v3.0: Full ecosystem status — 8 repos, 964+ assertions, zero open bugs"
category = "cyrius"
---
CYRIUS 3.0 ECOSYSTEM:

DOWNSTREAM REPOS (all compile + test clean on 3.0):
  agnostik:    223 assertions  (shared types, domain primitives)
  agnosys:     compiles clean  (20 modules, system foundation)
  argonaut:    395 assertions  (service orchestration, 15 test suites)
  sakshi:      12 assertions   (tracing/logging)
  majra:       144 assertions  (pub/sub, queues, SHA-1, base64, WebSocket)
  libro:       202 assertions  (audit chain, SHA-256, Merkle, streaming)
  bsp:         74 assertions   (geometry library)
  cyrius-doom: compiles (129KB binary)

CI STANDARDIZED:
  All repos use identical install pattern:
    curl tarball → $HOME/.cyrius/bin + lib → GITHUB_PATH
  All pinned to released version. cyrb→cyrius rename complete.
  cyrius.toml manifest in all projects (was cyrb.toml).

TOOLCHAIN:
  cyrius build/test/fuzz/soak/watch/deps/audit
  cyriusup for version management
  cyrfmt/cyrlint/cyrdoc/cyrc for quality

TOTAL: 964+ assertions across 5 tested repos. 8/8 compile clean.
  Soak test: self-host + tests + fuzz + 6 repos in loop.

[[entries]]
name = "cyrb_to_cyrius_complete"
description = "v2.3-v3.0: cyrb→cyrius rename fully propagated across all repos"
category = "cyrius"
---
The build tool rename from cyrb to cyrius took multiple releases to fully propagate:

v2.3.2: Core rename — scripts/cyrb → scripts/cyrius, programs/cyrb.cyr → programs/cyrius.cyr
v2.3.3: Version manager separated to cyriusup (was stomping on build tool)
v2.6.2-2.7.2: Downstream CI bumped (majra, sakshi, bsp, doom, argonaut)
v2.7.4-2.7.5: Last cyrb references cleaned from agnosys, argonaut, sakshi CI
v2.7.5: cyrb.toml → cyrius.toml in agnosys and argonaut

LESSON: Renames propagate slowly through an ecosystem. Each repo needs:
  1. CI workflow files updated (install pattern + tool names)
  2. Manifest file renamed
  3. CLAUDE.md references updated
  4. Version pinned to release that HAS the renamed binary

The toolchain tarball must include the new name — downstream repos that
pin to an old release will still get cyrb (which no longer exists).

[[entries]]
name = "stdlib_distribution_pattern"
description = "v3.2: Stdlib distribution pattern — single-file snapshots of modular repos"
category = "cyrius"
---
PATTERN: Modular repos distribute as single-file stdlib snapshots.

  sakshi repo (7 modules, 769 lines)
    → lib/sakshi.cyr (202 lines, slim profile)
    → lib/sakshi_full.cyr (489 lines, full profile)

  patra repo (9 modules, 2496 lines)
    → lib/patra.cyr (2496 lines, single file)

WORKFLOW:
  1. Develop in the repo (tests, benchmarks, CI)
  2. Tag a release (e.g., sakshi 0.8.0, patra 0.8.0)
  3. Copy distribution file to cyrius lib/
  4. Run cyrius lint/fmt/doc to verify
  5. Bump cyrius version, commit

CONSUMERS: just `include "lib/patra.cyr"` — no dep management, no network.
Installed with the toolchain. Available everywhere.

TRADEOFF: Distribution files lag behind repo HEAD. Consumers get
stable snapshots, not bleeding edge. Manual sync required.

